From bf82df27d7d5eaea9b913ec46a1381a712bcfd54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:26:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: a display connected while Kodi runs is taken
 without a restart on the same card

The display watch (CDRMDisplayWatch) asks when a display is connected that
a start of Kodi would take over the output in use, and started Kodi again
to take it: the interface went back to the home screen, what played
stopped, and the screen was dark for the length of a start. A change of the
Monitor setting already moves Kodi to another output of its graphics card
while it runs (CWinSystemGbm::MoveOutputTo(): the driver asked first, one
commit, Kodi back where it was if the driver refuses). The display watch
now takes a display that way too, where it can end only as a start would:

- the display is on an output of the graphics device in use, and a start
  would take that very output (HowToTake() of DRMOutputChoice.h, MoveFor()
  of the same look at the outputs);
- Kodi shows itself on an output (it has a CRTC and a plane to move) and
  its display is awake;
- the output carries the interface alone and no video plays (the
  conditions of a change of the Monitor setting); music only where the
  user said to move: a start would have ended it;
- a start would still take that output with the outputs as the kernel has
  them when the move is made (OutputMoveFor()).

The question is the same, at the same times, and nobody answering moves
Kodi under the same rules (nothing plays, not more often than before); its
text no longer says that Kodi restarts (#41070, #41071, in place of #39984
and #39980; translated in Estuary as #33170, #33171). Kodi stays where the
user was, and music that plays goes on.

Fail-closed: anything else, and a move that cannot be made, that the driver
refuses (Kodi is then back on the output it was on) or that loses both
outputs, ends in the start of Kodi of before, which takes the display.
Another graphics device always does: what Kodi draws with lives in the
device in use (its EGL display and OpenGL context, the buffers of its
window, every texture of the interface, the decoders that hand it their
pictures), and none of it can be made anew on another device while Kodi
runs. So does Kodi on no output, and a display of Kodi that sleeps.

kodi.log, at info level unless a warning is said:
  "Offer: a display was connected to HDMI-A-1 (amdgpu), which Kodi would use
   rather than eDP-1: the user is asked (Kodi moves there while it runs)"
   (or "... (a start of Kodi takes it: <why>)")
  "TakeDisplay: Kodi shows itself on HDMI-A-1 (amdgpu) now, moved while it
   runs in <n> ms"
  "Offer: the user said to move: Kodi shows itself on HDMI-A-1 (amdgpu),
   without a restart"
  "TakeDisplay: Kodi does not move to HDMI-A-1 (amdgpu) while it runs
   (<why>): it is started again, which takes it" (a warning where the
   driver refused the move or both outputs were lost)
and the lines of MoveOutputTo() ("Monitor: Kodi moves from eDP-1 to
HDMI-A-1 (a display was connected to it) ...").

Host test (outside the tree): the cases by hand (a laptop and its TV on one
card and on two, Kodi on no output, the output named in the settings, two
TVs that take Dolby Vision on two cards, what a look does not know, the
same output name on two cards) with the reason given for each, and 200000
random looks: a display taken while Kodi runs is always the output a start
would take, on the device in use; one of another device or with Kodi on no
output never is; and none that could be taken so is left to a start.
168588 checks; 12 mutations of the decision, each caught. The tests of the
choice of the output (131 checks, 13 mutations) and of the monitor to keep
(17 mutations) pass unchanged.
